.TH "GIT-FOREST" "1" "2026-08-10" "git-forest" "Git Manual"
.SH NAME
git-forest \- manage named workspaces across multiple Git repositories
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B git forest
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ command ]
.br
.B git-forest
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ command ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B git-forest
manages one named workspace across multiple configured canonical repositories
using linked Git worktrees.
Git exposes the installed executable as both
.B git-forest
and
.BR "git forest" .
.PP
With no command, Forest opens an interactive workspace launcher.
The
.B open
command starts the same launcher explicitly.
Without an interactive terminal, invoking Forest without a command prints its
command-line help.
.PP
Forest treats Git worktree metadata and the filesystem as authoritative.
It does not maintain a separate registry, reset or delete branches, or perform
implicit network operations.
Only
.BR setup ,
.BR fetch ,
and
.B update
contact remotes.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Display help.
When using the Git-style command, Git handles
.B git forest \-\-help
itself by opening this manual page.
Use
.B git forest \-h
for concise command-line help.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Display the git-forest version.
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Use an explicit configuration file instead of configuration discovery.
This option is global and may be used with any command.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Emit structured JSON.
This option is available on non-interactive commands.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B open
Search, create, and open workspaces interactively.
.TP
.B setup
Ensure configured canonical repositories are cloned.
Existing Git worktrees are reused without fetching or changing their remotes.
.TP
.B repos
List configured repositories, their origin URLs, and their locally known
default refs.
.TP
.BI fetch " [repository ...]"
Fetch
.B origin
for all configured repositories or for the named subset.
Use
.BI \-\-jobs " N"
to limit concurrent fetches.
.TP
.BI update " [repository ...]"
Fetch and fast-forward canonical default branches for all configured
repositories or for the named subset.
Updates are fast-forward only and never reset or force a branch.
Use
.BI \-\-jobs " N"
to limit concurrent fetches.
.TP
.BI create " workspace checkout ..."
Create a workspace and linked worktrees.
A checkout is
.B repository
for its primary worktree or
.B repository@slot
for an additional worktree from the same repository.
Use
.BI \-\-base " checkout=ref"
to override a creation base and
.BI \-\-branch " checkout=branch"
to select an existing local branch or create one from its remote-tracking ref.
Creation preflights every requested checkout before mutation and is safe to
repeat after partial execution.
.TP
.BI add " workspace checkout ..."
Add primary or named linked worktrees to an existing workspace.
It accepts the same
.B \-\-base
and
.B \-\-branch
options as
.BR create .
.TP
.B list
List workspaces by reconciling workspace directories with Git worktree
metadata.
.TP
.BI status " [workspace]"
Show workspace and worktree status, including dirty state, upstream, and
ahead/behind counts.
.TP
.BI path " workspace"
Print the absolute path of an existing workspace.
.TP
.BI attach " workspace"
Open an existing workspace in a running Herdr session.
.TP
.BI remove " workspace [checkout ...]"
Remove all worktrees in a workspace or only the named checkouts.
Removal rejects dirty or unregistered paths, uses
.B git worktree remove
for present worktrees and stale registrations whose paths are already missing,
preserves branches, and has no force option.
.TP
.BI completions " shell"
Generate dynamic shell completion setup for
.BR bash ,
.BR elvish ,
.BR fish ,
.BR powershell ,
or
.BR zsh .
This command does not require a Forest configuration.
.SH SHELL COMPLETION
Evaluate the generated setup from the shell startup file.
For Bash or Zsh:
.PP
.EX
source <(git-forest completions bash)
.EE
.PP
Use
.B zsh
instead of
.B bash
for Zsh.
For Fish:
.PP
.EX
git-forest completions fish | source
.EE
.PP
Bash, Zsh, and Fish complete both
.B git-forest
and
.BR "git forest" .
Dynamic candidates include configured repositories, existing workspaces, and
workspace checkouts where appropriate.
Completion honors normal configuration discovery,
.BR FOREST_CONFIG ,
and
.BR \-\-config .
It never contacts remotes, mutates worktrees, or invokes Herdr.
Regenerate the setup after upgrading Forest.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Forest reads a
.B .forest.toml
file with a supported version, configured repositories, and workspace path and
branch templates.
Unless
.B \-\-config
or
.B FOREST_CONFIG
is set, Forest searches from the current directory toward the filesystem root.
.PP
Paths in the file are relative to the directory containing it.
The repository remote template may use
.BR {name} ,
and the workspace branch template may use
.B {workspace}
and
.BR {checkout} .
The latter is the named slot when present and otherwise the workspace name.
See the git-forest README for the complete format and policy.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B FOREST_CONFIG
Path to the configuration file.
It has lower precedence than
.BR \-\-config .
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Disable colored human-readable output.
.TP
.B PATH
Used to find Git and, for
.BR attach ,
the Herdr executable.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, including fully idempotent operations.
.TP
.B 1
An operational conflict or a Git, Herdr, or filesystem failure.
.TP
.B 2
A usage, input, or configuration error.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR git (1),
.BR git-worktree (1)
